#{[17592186419785 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-3,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,0,-4⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186419786 "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $-5\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((6\\mathbf{b})\\times (4\\mathbf{c}))=120.$\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186419787 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,-1,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨5,-2,-2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186421760 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=100.$"] [17592186421761 "![](images/62/62c8f6d7db345ce76bd164bb52c13e5edef9c04f18f4f07501c8694e7f64b554.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $x={e}^{y}$ from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(e,1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186421762 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186421763 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186421764 "![](images/95/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186423120 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=-x-y$ for $-2\\le x\\le 2$ and $-2\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186423121 "For a surface $S$ given by $z={x}^{2}y$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}xy+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+R)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186423122 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\hspace{0.222em}Q,\\hspace{0.222em}R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂y}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186423123 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨(x+y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}(x-y)^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=y-x$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $-3\\le y\\le 0.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186423124 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)=⟨x+z,\\hspace{0.222em}2z,\\hspace{0.222em}y+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x-3y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}v\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186424097 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$"] [17592186424226 "Given that $\\mathbf{F}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}?$"] [17592186424229 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{ln}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ \nEvaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(\\pi ,1).$"] [17592186424230 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(x+y)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(y+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$"] [17592186424514 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}x+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0.$"] [17592186424515 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨14xy,\\hspace{0.222em}6{x}^{2}y+\\operatorname{sin}x⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186424516 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+y,\\hspace{0.222em}xy+1⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y,$ $\\,x=\\sqrt{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186424517 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{7}-x,\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{3x}+x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1),(0,-1),(0,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186424518 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y{e}^{y}-4xy,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi x)-4x{y}^{3}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,-1),(1,-1),(1,1),$ and $(0,1).$"] [17592186424519 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,-1),(1,-1),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(6xy+y{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186424520 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨-x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}2{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=-y+1,$ $\\,x={e}^{y},$ and the line $y=1.$"] [17592186424521 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨2+x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3-{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424522 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{4}+{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}({x}^{3}+5)y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(2,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186424523 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+8x{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186424524 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(0,-2),$ and $(1,-1)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186425876 "![](images/01/014c2310bcac5b6e9c2be2107bf8a5613b689ff29a8018d2be74fc607dd996db.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨7-2{t}^{3},3{t}^{2}+10⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=\\sqrt{3}.$"] [17592186430947 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x-z+2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-4y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{z}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}=\n$$"] [17592186430948 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=y{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430949 "![](images/6d/6dc4f6d9e8520dead79789efd4f08c07ea7921d28f4cf6f8da06de533d012e90.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=⟨y-z,\\hspace{0.222em}y-2x,\\hspace{0.222em}x+4z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $S.$"] [17592186430950 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}=(y+{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430951 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(yz-2x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(x+z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$"] [17592186430952 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186431049 "![](images/a9/a95b161dab89fe1245724921ef4ee36112d7a4062e7e171411e4ffe61253b91f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186431051 "![](images/b5/b51e252935f7077f3285258845d8d1e4957b83894037f968ee306910aa88bad6.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$"] [17592186431052 "![](images/9e/9ebabcf1e3c3c3d2f4850d425657e7314c0135c81a432bfbbc8ae31ee39cb85d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-2{t}^{4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${\\mathbf{r}}_{2}(t)=-t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186431053 "![](images/10/10f4094edb09afc7908bd0cd3df089387c8f5b762ea5ef86da152d1c9d08b5e4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186431348 "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=4,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-6,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$"] [17592186431350 "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=2,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=9,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$"] [17592186431351 "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=4,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=9,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$"] [17592186431357 "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=7,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-3,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$"] [17592186436855 "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-5,7⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨8,-2,-4⟩.$"] [17592186436856 "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,0,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-4,9⟩.$"] [17592186436857 "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,4,2⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186436858 "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,5,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,3,2⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}"] [1